NOTE: To read changes made to the Debian package (since
August 23rd 2001) see the changelog.Debian file

Changes since 3.4.0 (unreleased)
--------------------------------

- tigris --root DIR audits a system that is not running: a disk mounted
  from another machine, a container image unpacked into a directory, a
  VM snapshot. Its files are read under DIR through util/rootpath, which
  follows its symbolic links inside it (an absolute link in an image
  does not lead to the host's files, and .. stops at DIR), and the
  host's package tools are pointed at its databases: dpkg --admindir,
  apt through APT_CONFIG (its apt.conf.d, not the host's, with the
  host's dpkg named on the command line and no cache or log written),
  rpm --root (chrooted, without verify scripts), dnf --installroot,
  zypper --root, apk --root, pacman --sysroot. Nothing from the image
  is run. A check marks itself "# Tigris: offline" when it can read an
  offline root: so far pkg_integrity, deb_checkmd5sums, deb_nopackfiles,
  deb_statoverride, check_updates (without the reboot question),
  check_pam, check_ssh, check_sudo, the account checks, cron and
  check_units. Those marked "# Tigris: live system" (processes, sockets,
  the firewall, sysctl, mounts, Secure Boot, ...) are skipped with that
  reason, and the rest as "not offline yet"; each skip is in the report
  and in the JSON, and neither counts against the exit status. The run
  record carries the root, so tigris-diff counts it as part of the
  configuration. A --root without an etc directory, or with characters
  the checks would misread (a space, a quote), stops the run (con014e).
  tests/deb_checks.sh (Debian, Ubuntu) and tests/pkg_checks.sh (Fedora,
  Rocky 9, openSUSE Tumbleweed, Alpine, Arch) now also audit a copy of
  the container's root, and the package checks must report the same of
  the copy as of the system; tests/offline_check.sh covers the rest.
  Mounted read-only, dnf 4 and zypper cannot answer (they must write
  into the root) and upd004i quotes them.

- The account checks read an offline root: check_passwd, check_group,
  check_passwdformat, check_passwdspec, check_accounts, check_root,
  check_issue, check_rootdir and check_path, with the password and group
  sets built from the root's own files (NIS and LDAP, which would be
  asked over the network, are left out). getpermit and pathmsg, which
  most of the old checks go through, take the root's paths, resolve them
  inside it and name owners and groups from its passwd and group files,
  so a file owned by uid 1000 is reported as the image's user 1000, not
  this host's. Neither this host's pwck, grpck, visudo nor chage is run
  on an image's files (pwck -R would chroot, and glibc can then load the
  image's NSS modules: the image's code, as root); check_passwdformat
  runs in pwck's place.

- Security: check_path sourced NAME=value lines taken from users' dot
  files to learn their PATH, and its filters let a redirection through,
  so "PATH=x>/etc/passwd" in any user's .profile truncated /etc/passwd
  when Tigris ran as root with Tiger_Check_PATHALL=Y (or root's own
  .profile, always). Only assignments without whitespace, redirections,
  pipes, &, ;, parentheses or backquotes are sourced now.

- pass019w (password aging off) never fired: it looked for "Password
  Expires:" and "Never" in chage's output, which chage has not printed in
  years. It is worked out from the shadow file now, as chage does, and
  accounts locked with "!*" (systemd's) or "!!" (Fedora) are not counted.
  A system whose users have no maximum password age reports them again.

- acc005w (a disabled account with a crontab) never looked where Debian
  keeps crontabs, /var/spool/cron/crontabs.

- check_passwdformat accepts names that start with an underscore
  (Debian's _apt and _ssh), contain dots or end in $ (Samba's machine
  accounts); its group pattern's A-z also took [, \, ], ^ and the
  backquote. Without column it prints the duplicates one a line instead
  of stopping.

- check_units reads an offline root: systemctl cat refuses --root, so
  units are read with systemd-analyze --root cat-config, which gives the
  unit file and its drop-ins as systemctl cat does and resolves links
  inside the root; an instance (getty@tty1) is read as its template.

- cron: gen_cron and check_crontabs rewritten. /etc/crontab and
  /etc/cron.d, where most of a system's cron jobs are today, were never
  read; now they are, with the cron.hourly ... cron.monthly scripts and
  both spool layouts (Debian's /var/spool/cron/crontabs, Red Hat's and
  Arch's /var/spool/cron). Each file cron reads is reported when others
  can write it (cron008f, cron008w) or it is not root's, or for a user's
  crontab its user's (cron009w), and a spool crontab named after no
  account is reported (cron010w): cron runs it as soon as an account of
  that name exists. gen_cron's warnings were bare text without an id and
  never reached the JSON. Jobs with "--" in their command were dropped by
  the filter for that text, so "run-parts --report" and the like were
  never checked; shell builtins (test, [, true) no longer count as
  commands without a full path; a job's home comes from passwd, not from
  getuserhome's bash -c "echo ~NAME"; and on a system without cron
  nothing is said, where "Use of cron is not restricted" used to be.
  All of it reads an offline root, owners named by its passwd.

- check_ssh reads an offline root too: sshd is not run there (it would
  be the host's, with the host's keys), so the effective configuration
  is worked out from the root's sshd_config as sshd would: Include
  files in place, relative ones from /etc/ssh, the first value of a
  keyword kept, Match blocks set aside, Keyword=value, the old
  ChallengeResponseAuthentication name, LoginGraceTime in minutes, and
  OpenSSH's defaults for the rest (no UsePAM where sshd has no PAM
  stack, as on Alpine). Then it is checked as sshd -T's output is. On
  Debian, Fedora, Arch, openSUSE Tumbleweed and Alpine with
  openssh-server installed, the findings from a copy of the root are
  the findings sshd -T gives on the system.

- check_sudo reads an offline root too: its sudoers is flattened first,
  every @include and @includedir (and #include, #includedir) read
  inside the root as sudo reads them (relative paths from the including
  file's directory; a directory's files in lexical order, without those
  ending in ~ or with a dot), and this host's cvtsudoers reads the
  result, so it does not follow the includes on this host. On Debian,
  Fedora, Arch, Alpine and openSUSE Tumbleweed with sudo installed, the
  findings from a copy of the root are those from the system.

- check_sudo reads openSUSE's /usr/etc/sudoers, which sudo reads when
  there is no /etc/sudoers (Tumbleweed ships none), and its sudoers.d:
  until now a Tumbleweed system's sudo rules went unchecked.

- deb_checkmd5sums checks every package with one md5sum run instead of
  one per package: on the reference machine 17.5 s instead of 20.5,
  and 3.3 s of system time instead of 14, with the same findings.

- upd004i quotes dnf's or zypper's own error instead of guessing that
  the cache is empty.

Changes (v 3.4.0)
--------------------

Twelve new checks for how a Linux system is run today (Secure Boot,
AppArmor and SELinux, auditd, time, updates, sudo, PAM, systemd units,
storage, containers, the firewall and the ports Docker publishes past
it), profiles, a non-root mode that says what it skipped, and one
metadata file per finding id with a category in the JSON.

On the reference machine (Linux Mint 22.3, kernel 7.0), as root with
nothing else running: a full run takes 1:58 (107 s user, 92 s system)
and writes 306 lines (13 FAIL, 61 WARN); the quick profile takes 1:02.
Peak memory is 134 MB in both, for under a second, from apt-get mapping
its package cache when check_updates asks what is waiting (file-backed
pages, shared with apt itself). Without check_updates the quick run
peaks at 32 MB, the docker CLI that check_containers asks; 3.3.0 peaked
at 34.6 MB full and 13.9 MB quick.

Incompatible changes

- The exit status is the worst finding of the run: 0 nothing at WARN or
  above, 2 a check could not run (an ERROR, or a check skipped because
  it needs root), 3 WARN, 4 FAIL, 5 ALERT; 1 still means the audit did
  not run. It was 0 for every audit that finished, so a script that
  treats anything else as a failure has to change. -q now silences the
  run itself, not only the banner.

- Nine ids reported through pathmsg carry the letter of their level, as
  every other id does and the JSON schema requires: ali003, ali007 and
  cron002 become ...w or ...i; ali004, ali008, cron003 and suid002
  become ...f, ...w or ...i; path006 and path007 become path011w/i and
  path012f/w/i (path006w and path007w meant other things before). A
  3.3.0 report compared with a 3.4.0 one shows those findings resolved
  and new once, and an accept file that names the old ids needs the new
  ones.

- Explanations live in meta/ID, one file per finding id, instead of
  doc/*.txt and the index util/genmsgidx built (both gone, as are
  util/doc2html and util/convert2html). tigexp keeps its options and
  reads them directly; nothing needs rebuilding as root. The
  explanations of the 210 ids nothing emits any more are in git, under
  doc/*.txt at the tag version_3_3_0.

- Run as an ordinary user, the checks that need root are skipped
  instead of run (see below): a user's run reports less, and exits 2.

- check_listeningprocs reports IPv6 listeners, which it never saw
  before: the first 3.4.0 run on a host with services on [::] shows
  them as new.

- lin019f is gone: check_firewall replaces it (fire001w to fire004e).

New checks

- check_firewall: is incoming traffic denied unless something accepts
  it, for IPv4 and IPv6, read from the kernel's ruleset whatever wrote
  it (nft, iptables-nft, ufw, firewalld, legacy iptables): fire001w to
  fire004e. And the ports Docker publishes, which are forwarded before
  the input chain: with ufw enabled, a listener on the host did not
  answer from outside and a published port did (fire005w, fire006i).

- check_secureboot: Secure Boot off, in setup mode or on, and a kernel
  Secure Boot verified that is not locked down (boot009w to boot013e,
  lin040w).

- check_mac: AppArmor or SELinux enforcing, permissive, or absent, with
  AppArmor's profiles counted by mode (mac001w to mac004e).

- check_audit: auditd running with rules, and logs kept across reboots,
  journald's Storage= resolved as systemd resolves it or a syslog
  daemon (aud001w to aud004e, logf008w).

- check_timesync: a time daemon, one only, and the clock synchronised,
  asked of chronyc for chrony (time001w to time004w). check_ntp stays
  for an ntpd's configuration.

- check_updates, offline: security updates waiting from what apt, dnf
  or zypper has cached, whether they are installed automatically
  (unattended-upgrades, Linux Mint's update automation, dnf-automatic),
  and a reboot pending (upd001w to upd004i). check_patches, which goes
  to the network and is off by default, stays.

- check_sudo, from cvtsudoers: NOPASSWD for every command or for some,
  !authenticate, wildcards, sudoers files not root's alone (sudo001w to
  sudo006e). check_pam: no password quality module, a minimum length
  under 8, no lockout after failed logins (pam001w to pam003w).

- check_units: what enabled systemd services, and the services their
  timers and sockets start, run: their programs and every directory
  above them, owned and writable as cron's commands are checked
  (sysd004, sysd005), their unit files root's alone (sysd006f), and
  which are local or changed locally (sysd007i, sysd008i).

- check_storage: /tmp and /dev/shm without nosuid, nodev or noexec, /tmp
  not separate, disks not encrypted (a WARN on a machine with a battery
  of its own), swap unencrypted under an encrypted root, core dumps as
  files, and USB storage where Tiger_Storage_NoUSB=Y forbids it
  (stor001w to stor008w).

- check_containers: who may use the Docker or Podman socket (root in
  effect), the Docker API on TCP without TLS, dockerd as root, and
  running containers that are privileged, share the host's PID or
  network namespace, or have the engine's socket or the host's / or
  /etc mounted (cont001w to cont010e).

- deb_statoverride: files that differ from what dpkg-statoverride sets
  for them (lin039w), the only modes dpkg records. lin038w stays rpm,
  apk and pacman only.

Checks improved

- check_listeningprocs: IPv6, from ss when there is one, then lsof,
  netstat or /proc itself (Alpine without iproute2 has only busybox's
  lsof and netstat, which it read as nothing); the user is each
  process's effective uid, as lsof had it; a process's ephemeral UDP
  ports are one finding with the ports in the detail.

- check_suid and check_sgid compare with what the installed packages
  ship setuid or setgid where the package manager records modes (rpm,
  pacman, apk), rather than Tigris's static list.

- pathmsg passes over a sticky directory above a path (/tmp), which
  lets nobody replace what is not theirs.

Running

- tigris --profile NAME (or -p): a short file in profiles/ read on top
  of the tigerrc. quick (no filesystem scan, the same as tigerrc-quick,
  which stays), server, desktop, container; a site's own go in a
  profiles directory beside its tigerrc.

- tigris --since RUN|last prints only what changed since that run and
  takes its exit status from the new findings: tigris -q --since last
  in cron mails only when something changed.

- Without root, a check whose header says "# Tigris: needs root" is
  skipped: a line in the report, a skip record in the JSON, a count in
  the summary. Twelve checks are marked: nine whose answers as a user
  were false or missing on the reference machine, and check_audit,
  pkg_integrity and check_finddeleted, which cannot read what they need
  without root.

- tigris explain ID shows what a finding means, with its severity,
  category and the checks that report it.

Output

- Every finding in the JSON carries its category, one of 15 (accounts,
  boot, containers, cron, filesystem, firewall, integrity, intrusion,
  kernel, logging, network, packages, services, ssh, tigris). The run
  record names the profile; the summary counts skipped checks; skip
  records are new. tigris-diff leaves a check skipped in either run out
  of both sides, and counts a different profile as a different
  configuration. All of it additions to schema 1.

- make builds doc/explanations.html, one page of every finding by
  category.

Engineering

- 27 fixture suites in tests/ (12 in 3.3.0), each with a CI step; the
  new ones pass under mawk, gawk and busybox awk. ShellCheck blocks the
  build at the error level.

Changes (v 3.3.0)
--------------------

This is the first release of Tigris, the independent fork of Tiger at
https://github.com/creativeheadz/tigris. The entry point is ./tigris;
tigerrc, the Tiger_* settings, the tiger command and /etc/tiger keep
their names until 4.0.

On the reference machine (Linux Mint 22.3, kernel 7.0, 2.4k packages,
a 1.8 TB and a 3.6 TB disk), a full run as root under 3.2.4 took 7:16
and 159 MB, and wrote a report of 49,651 lines in which 24 checks had
refused to run. Under 3.3.0 it takes 1:58 and 34.6 MB and writes 218
lines (12 FAIL, 50 WARN); the quick profile takes 0:58 and 13.9 MB.

Runs properly on a modern Linux

- config: on a kernel newer than the version symlinks, the fallback to
  systems/Linux/default left REL set to the real version, so every
  Linux-specific check silently did not run and the generic ones ran
  in their place. The fallback now sets REL=default.

- ./tigris runs from a checkout with sudo: the tree-ownership guard
  accepts the sudo user's own checkout, and explains itself (init010e)
  when it refuses. The log directory is created when missing; the rc
  preprocessor uses mktemp.

- scripts/find_files: one find with -fprintf classifies every file in
  a single pass, instead of an ls | awk per file (about a million
  processes on the reference machine). The old path stays for busybox.
  Dangling symlinks and unusual file names are listed one by one only
  under system directories (Tiger_FSScan_DetailDirs) and summarised per
  directory elsewhere (fsys016w, fsys017w). Flatpak, Docker,
  containerd, Podman and snap stores are pruned by default, as are a
  pruned directory's own mount points.

- Checks brought up to date with how systems look now: PATH and umask
  set through pam_env, pam_umask and systemd; merged-/usr; service
  accounts with nologin and no home; /var/log/syslog and the journal;
  /dev as udev builds it, including systemd's inaccessible placeholder
  nodes; su and passwd being setuid; the duplicate-port test against
  the system's own services file.

- egrep, tempfile and which are no longer called. util/realpath is a
  shell stand-in for the C helper when that is not built. The
  distribution is read from /etc/os-release, as text, never sourced.

- The checks for services that are gone from a current Linux are
  retired: inetd, xinetd and tcpd, rhosts and .netrc, anonymous FTP and
  ftpusers, printcap, OmniBack, NIS+, inittab, and the static Debian
  advisory list (17 files). The boot check stays; it covers GRUB 2.

- The configurations for AIX, HP-UX, IRIX, NeXT, SunOS, Tru64, UNICOS
  and Mac OS X moved out of the tree (364 files). They are in the git
  history under the tag attic/other-unix-2026-10.

- A dead network mount no longer hangs the audit. lsof runs with -b,
  and every home directory, and every mount point before the scan, is
  asked first with df under a kill timeout (Tiger_Mount_Timeout, 5 s);
  one that does not answer is reported (acc025w, path010w, con011c)
  and left alone.

- check_path: the switch to check every user's PATH was read as
  TIGER_Check_PATHALL, which nothing sets, so since 2002 only root's
  PATH was checked. It now reads Tiger_Check_PATHALL, as tigerrc
  spells it.

- check_services, check_passwd, check_accounts, check_devices and
  check_logfiles fixed for false positives found on the reference
  machine; two assignments that ran as commands fixed; eight scripts
  marked executable.

New checks

- systems/Linux/2/check_sysctl: kernel pointer and log restriction,
  Yama, ASLR, SysRq, BPF, perf, fs.protected_*, IPv4 and IPv6 redirects
  and source routing, and the kernel's own verdict on CPU speculation
  flaws (lin020w to lin035f).

- check_ssh reads the effective configuration with sshd -T: 15
  directives and the cipher, MAC and key-exchange lists (ssh006f to
  ssh019w).

- systems/Linux/2/check_systemd: sandboxing of network-facing services,
  scored by systemd-analyze security, naming the three unmet
  protections that would help most (sysd001w to sysd003i).

- systems/Linux/2/pkg_integrity: package integrity for rpm, apk and
  pacman with the finding ids the dpkg checks use (lin005f, lin006f,
  lin001w), plus lin038w for a mode or owner that differs from the
  package. Honours pacman's NoExtract and NoUpgrade.

- deb_checkmd5sums: one grep for the diversions instead of a dpkg -S
  each (130 MB to 11 MB), and diversions matched without their
  architecture, so multi-arch packages are checked again.

Output and drift

- A JSON Lines report next to the text report, written by message()
  itself: a run record (schema 1, run id, tigerrc used, whether the
  filesystem scan ran), one record per finding, and a summary with the
  counts. The contract is doc/json-format.md, with a JSON Schema in
  doc/tigris-report.schema.json.

- tigris-diff: what is new, resolved and unchanged between two runs,
  in text or JSON (-j), exit 1 when anything is new. When one of the
  runs skipped the filesystem scan, its findings are set aside rather
  than reported as resolved.

- tigris-accept: acknowledge a finding with a reason and an expiry
  date. It leaves the text report, is marked as accepted in the JSON,
  and comes back (acpt001w) when the date passes.

- tigerrc-quick: everything except the filesystem scan, in about a
  minute. The text report says the scan was skipped.

- Report file names carry seconds, so two runs in the same minute no
  longer overwrite each other.

- Every finding id is well-formed and explained: 308 emitted, 308 with
  an entry for tigexp. Placeholder ids replaced (tig001e, suid002,
  pass022w) and 22 malformed ones renumbered.

Engineering

- 12 fixture suites in tests/, plain sh, a full-run smoke test, and a
  CI pipeline of 11 jobs: syntax and ShellCheck, a full run on Ubuntu,
  the dpkg checks on Debian stable, sid and Ubuntu 24.04, and the
  package checks on Fedora, Rocky Linux 9, openSUSE Tumbleweed, Alpine
  and Arch.

- ROADMAP.md, SECURITY.md, CONTRIBUTING.md and issue templates.

- tigerrc-all and tigerrc-dist, which still carried the switches of
  retired checks and which nothing installed, are dropped.

Changes (v 3.2.4)
--------------------

- Fixed some bashisms in files:
   * systems/default/check_ndd, line 90.
   * systems/HPUX/check_passwdspec, lines 101, 108.

- Fixed a problem in the sh script file scripts/sub/check_suid
  finding the owner of files. 

- Fixed warning messages in scripts/sub/check_nousrgrp to make them
  easier to ignore and add proper documentation for the warnings

- Updated mailing lists info in README

- Fixed a problem in delete() function to remove properly files in loop
  in initdef script.

- Fixed a problem removing a temporary file in scripts/find_file script.

- scripts/check_known: Fixed problems handling files with spaces or
  newline characters in their filename in /*/lost+found/ directories.

- Added support for recognize simfs as local filesystem.

- Improve documentation for tiger messages

- Fixed a bashism problem with logical expression in /systems/HPUX/check_trusted

- Added support to recognize fuse.ltspfs for ltspfs file system and ltsp-server
  in systems/Linux/2/gen_mounts script.

- Added a 'default' system directory for Linux, pointing to version 2, so
  that any kernel version is checked with the Linux 2 configuration.

- The output of pwck -r and grpck is now part of the report (Debian
  bug #512076).

- Dormant accounts are only reported for non-system accounts, as proposed
  by Richard Laager (Ubuntu bug 248858).

- Use 'command -v' instead of the deprecated which (Francois Marier,
  Debian bug #992655).

- Treat fuse.portal as a non-local file system (Francois Marier, Debian
  bug #987512).

- c/md5.c builds with GCC 15 (Richard Lewis, Debian bug #1097997).

- Makefile.in: every install target now depends on installdirs, so
  parallel installs work (Richard Lewis, Debian bug #1105709).

- systems/Linux/2/deb_nopackfiles: support merged-/usr systems. The check
  no longer reports every file in /bin, /sbin and /lib as unowned, and it
  accepts files that dpkg records under either the /bin or the /usr/bin
  form (Debian bug #1111306).

- systems/Linux/2/deb_checkmd5sums:
   * Stop using prelink, which was called with options it rejects
     (Debian bug #505906, Savannah bug #52087).
   * Parse current md5sum output, so missing package files are reported
     again and file names with spaces are handled.
   * Actually exclude /usr/share/doc, man, locale and the other
     /usr/share directories that are meant to be skipped.

- scripts/find_files: new tigerrc variable Tiger_FSScan_PruneDirs, a
  pipe-separated list of find -path patterns for directories the file
  system scan should skip. Based on a patch from Nicholas Bamber (Debian
  bug #610785).

- systems/Linux/2/gen_bootparam_sets: set SED rather than AWK when SED is
  unset.

- This release was made in an independent fork of Tiger at
  https://github.com/creativeheadz/tiger by Andrei Trimbitas. The official
  project remains on GNU Savannah, maintained by Javier Fernandez-Sanguino.


Changes (v 3.2.3) 
--------------------

- SECURITY FIX: Fix a temporary race condition in the genmsgidx script (only
  affects those that build the code in untrusted systems)

- Added support for more local and non-local filesystems in Linux gen_mounts:
  reiser4, securityfs, fuse.gvfs-fuse-daemon, fuseblk, fuse.truecrypt,
  fuse.encfs, debugfs, afs, configfs, gfs, gfs2, inotifyfs, hugetlb, subfs,
  futexfs and bind.

- Added a new configuration variable in tigerrc (Tiger_FSScan_WarnUnknown) that
  allows administrators to disable the warning related to unknown filesystems
  and modified gen_mounts to make use of it.

  The default behaviour (in the provided tigerrc and in the code), if the variable is not
  set is to warn admins since unknown filesystems might be a security issue (rootkit?)

- Add new variables in tigerrc:
   * Tiger_FSScan_Local: if set, filesystems defined in it will be considered
     local and will always be analysed.
   * Tiger_FSScan_NonLocal: ff set, filesystems defined in it will be
      considered non-local and will not be analysed.
  This allows administrators to add there esoteric filessystems in use so that they can
  work around the 'unknown filesystem' report generated by gen_mounts until it gets updated
  upstream.

- Fix the main Makefile so that the code gets compiled when running 'make all'

- Add a new Makefile to the doc/ subdirectory so that the contents get built and removed
  and no autogenerated content is distributed. This subdirectory is called from the main
  Makefile.

- Use tempfile if available in the configuration step, although we work in
  WORKDIR and we are safe there

- Make tigercron POSIX compliant so it works in any shell, not just bash

- The 'gen_cron' script for Linux now handles properly the case when the special @daily,@reboot,
  etc. definitions are used instead of real times. 

- Scripts changes:
    * check_apache:Fix the way the configuration file is handled to obtain
                    the IP address and port (Debian bug #436904)
    * check_crontags, check_xinetd, check_apache: Change message calls so that they can be filtered 
    * find_files: Only check local filesystems
    * systems/Linux/2/deb_checkmd5sums: use prelink if available


Changes (v 3.2.2) 
----------------
- Applied patches from Ryan Bradetich to fix Makefiles in HPUX
- Fixed bashisms in scripts
- Fixed YPCAT calls
- Added the audit scripts collection (audit/ subdir). These 
  scripts extract information for a given operating system 
  for offline review and have been provided by Marc Heuse and
  improved by me. For more information see the README file in
  that dir.
  Supported OSes:
    - AIX (tested in 4.x and 5.x)   - Debian GNU/Linux
    - HPUX 10/11                    - Nokia IPSO
    - ORACLE                        - RedHat GNU/Linux
    - Slackware                     - Solaris
    - SuSE Linux                    - Windows XP/2000/2003
- Added support for a check.d directory where administrators can dump
  scripts and have Tiger run them periodically
- Small improvements to the messages's documentation. Including fixes to
  some error Ids which were not correct
- Documentation improvements: explain new options and behaviour
  in the manpages, overall improvements to text files provided in sources.

- Fixed scripts: check_accounts, check_aliases, check_anonftp, check_crontabs,
  check_devices, check_embedded, check_exports, check_finddeleted,
  check_ftpusers, check_group, check_inetd, check_known, check_listeningprocs,
